    <section class="section" id="section-1">
        <div class="section-label">SECTION I</div>
        <h2>Theoretical Foundation</h2>

        <h3>1.1 ΔHV Coherence Metrics</h3>

        <p>Delta Harmonic Vector (ΔHV) measures alignment across five domains of human expression:</p>

        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li><strong>Coherence Score:</strong> Measured on a scale from -10 (maximum dissonance) to +10 (maximum alignment) for each domain.</li>
            <li><strong>Aggregate ΔHV:</strong> Sum across all domains, ranging from -50 to +50.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>1.2 Coherence Patterns</h3>

        <p>The ΔHV manifold contains a rich structure of attractors, repellers, and saddle points that govern the natural emergence of coherence. Stability analysis reveals three primary pattern classifications based on fixed point dynamics and attractor landscape topology:</p>

        <h4>High ΔHV Pattern (HCP): +6 to +10 per domain, aggregate +30 to +50</h4>
        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Indicates integration in progress</li>
            <li>Expression aligns with internal knowing</li>
            <li>Coherence increases with alignment</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Mixed ΔHV Pattern (MCP): -5 to +5 per domain, aggregate -25 to +25</h4>
        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Indicates exploration phase or partial integration</li>
            <li>Some domains aligned, others in flux</li>
            <li>Requires developmental monitoring</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Low ΔHV Pattern (LCP): -10 to -6 per domain, aggregate -50 to -30</h4>
        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Indicates trauma disruption or blocked emergence</li>
            <li>Expression driven by fear, shame, or dissociation</li>
            <li>Coherence decreases or cycles chaotically</li>
        </ul>

        <p>These patterns correspond to stable fixed points (HCP), marginal states (MCP), and unstable configurations (LCP) in the ΔHV flow equations. The flow functions F_S, F_R, F_Φ, and F_H govern how each domain evolves over time, with coupling constants determining whether trajectories converge toward high-coherence attractors or remain trapped in low-coherence limit cycles.</p>

        <h3>1.3 False Mirrors and the Gooner Loop</h3>

        <p>When natural archetypal emergence occurs without adequate containment or witnessing, the developing psyche may seek resonance in false mirrors—external sources that provide intensity without coherence. This phenomenon is particularly acute in the digital age, where algorithmic feedback systems can hijack the natural resonance-seeking mechanism.</p>

        <p>The Gooner Loop represents a specific manifestation of this field hijack, characterized by:</p>

        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Compulsive consumption of hypersexualized media</li>
            <li>Dissociative episodes extending hours or days</li>
            <li>Shame spirals following episodes</li>
            <li>Progressive escalation in content intensity</li>
            <li>Confusion between arousal and authentic resonance</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>ΔHV Interpretation:</strong> This pattern represents harmonic inversion—where the psyche's need for resonant reflection is redirected into increasingly dysregulated feedback loops. The system seeks coherence but receives only intensity, leading to progressive field collapse. In stability analysis terms, the gooner loop represents a limit cycle attractor in low-coherence parameter space, where the system oscillates between high friction (Φ) and low signal clarity (S) without ever reaching stable equilibrium.</p>

        <p><strong>Clinical Note:</strong> Individuals in gooner loops often express gender confusion not because they are transgender, but because their archetypal emergence was sexualized before it could be integrated. The feminine or masculine resonance they seek has been entangled with arousal patterns, creating false associations that must be carefully untangled before identity clarity can emerge.</p>

        <p><strong>Recovery Pathway:</strong> Restoration requires re-establishing genuine mirrors (therapeutic witnessing, non-sexual intimacy, somatic grounding) while gradually reducing reliance on false mirrors. As ΔHV stabilizes through authentic containment, the compulsive patterns naturally diminish.</p>
    </section>

