        <div class="section-label">SECTION II</div>
        <h2>Assessment Protocol</h2>

        <h3>2.1 Initial Assessment (Ages 5-12)</h3>

        <p><strong>Objective:</strong> Distinguish archetypal exploration from gender incongruence from trauma response.</p>

        <h4>Assessment Tools</h4>

        <p>These markers allow clinicians to map the current position in ΔHV parameter space and predict trajectory evolution. Longitudinal assessment tracks whether the system is flowing toward stable high-coherence attractors or cycling in low-coherence limit cycles, enabling developmentally appropriate intervention timing.</p>

        <h4>1. Somatic Markers</h4>
        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Body comfort/discomfort patterns</li>
            <li>Physical dysphoria vs. general disconnection</li>
            <li>Response to body-neutral vs. gendered clothing</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>2. Emotional Markers</h4>
        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Affect when expressing gender variance</li>
            <li>Relief vs. anxiety with different expressions</li>
            <li>Emotional stability over 6+ month observation</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>3. Cognitive Markers</h4>
        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Narrative consistency ('I am' vs. 'I wish' vs. 'I have to')</li>
            <li>Ability to hold ambiguity</li>
            <li>Obsessive vs. exploratory quality</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>4. Social Markers</h4>
        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Peer relationship quality across expressions</li>
            <li>Performance for adults vs. authentic play</li>
            <li>Comfort in gender-mixed vs. segregated contexts</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>5. Temporal Markers</h4>
        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Consistency across contexts (home, school, play)</li>
            <li>Duration of expressed identity (weeks vs. months vs. years)</li>
            <li>Response to environmental changes</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

