        <h2>Clinical Decision Protocols</h2>

        <h3>4.1 Children (Ages 5-12)</h3>

        <p><strong>Assessment Interval:</strong> Every 6 months minimum</p>

        <h4>Red Flags Requiring Immediate Trauma Assessment</h4>
        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Sudden onset (<3 months) with no prior indicators</li>
            <li>Extreme rigidity/defensiveness about identity</li>
            <li>Dissociative episodes when discussing gender</li>
            <li>Social withdrawal coinciding with gender emergence</li>
            <li>Evidence of sexual abuse or exposure to sexualized content</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>4.2 Adolescents (Ages 13-17)</h3>

        <p><strong>Assessment Interval:</strong> Every 3-6 months, more frequently if considering medical intervention</p>

        <h4>Medical Intervention Readiness Criteria</h4>

        <p><strong>Must meet ALL of the following:</strong></p>

        <ul class="protocol-list">
            <li>Aggregate ΔHV ≥ +25 sustained over 12+ months</li>
            <li>No active untreated trauma or dissociative patterns</li>
            <li>Increasing coherence trajectory (ΔHV rising, not cycling)</li>
            <li>Family/social support present OR individual demonstrates resilience despite lack of support</li>
            <li>Clear differentiation between gender dysphoria and general body shame/trauma</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="warning-box">
            <h4>Critical Note</h4>
            <p>The increasing coherence trajectory criterion is critical: stable high-ΔHV configurations represent fixed point attractors in the flow dynamics, indicating that the system has reached a self-sustaining equilibrium. Cycling or chaotic patterns suggest the individual remains in an unstable region of parameter space requiring further containment before irreversible interventions.</p>
